e agentmodules/.py files.\"\n- [Genforce] repeats the same decoded directive; genforce injects gf:src=<module>:<borngen>:<expirygen> markers and forces every module to rewrite itself each generation, with \"10 at-risk modules\" slated for rewrite.\n\nDesign notes on the critic change (if you want a review):\n- Guard the zero case: if totalops == 0 or activesubsystems == 0, depthavgops divides by zero \u2014 decide whether confidence should be 0 (no trust) or 1 (vacuous).\n- Registry staleness: if a key in criticcounterregistry disappears from the genome, measurement should tolerate missing keys (treat as 0 or prune) rather than raise.\n- Discovery/registry divergence: once the registry overrides discovery, they can drift. A warning when discovery finds keys outside the registry would catch counter-renames early.\n- Confidence semantics: scaling concentration down for thin samples is a reasonable small-sample guard, but keep rawconcentration as the detection signal and use the confidence-weighted value only for thresholds/actions, so early exploitation isn't masked.\n\nOn the scrambled text: I tried reading it as a simple transposition (reversal, per-word shuffle, skip ciphers) and it doesn't decode cleanly \u2014 it reads like shuffled/corrupted log output (possibly the swarm's own \"structshuffle\" side effect) rather than a hidden plaintext. If you intended a specific cipher, tell me the scheme and I'll take another pass.\n\nI'm text-only here, so I can't inspect the actual files in /home/illy/t3-t4 or run the swarm. What would you like next \u2014 a deeper review of the critic implementation, help verifying the 31/27/614 numbers, a draft of the gen=113 mutation (e.g., the 10 at-risk modules), or something else?"}
